# ─── Core ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PORT=3000
TZ=Asia/Kuala_Lumpur
LOG_LEVEL=info                    # debug | info | warn | error
DATABASE_PATH=/data/automator.db
RUN_RETENTION_DAYS=30             # 0 disables pruning

# Runs executing at once, across every workflow. Extra runs queue, they are
# never dropped. 0 = unlimited (what this was before the cap existed).
MAX_CONCURRENT_RUNS=10
SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_MS=20000         # how long SIGTERM waits for in-flight runs

# ─── Run data capture ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Records step inputs/outputs and every ctx.http request+response for the run
# page. Redacted through the secret filter; request headers are never stored.
CAPTURE_DATA=true
CAPTURE_MAX_BYTES=32768           # per captured value (step input, HTTP body)
CHECKPOINT_MAX_BYTES=262144       # per step output — resume replays these
                                  # also the ceiling on a recorded webhook body

# ─── Webhook inbox ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# An async webhook is written down before its 202 goes back, and finished on
# the next boot if a restart landed in between. Nothing to switch on; these
# only tune it. Unlike CAPTURE_DATA, which is observational, the inbox stores
# regardless — a privacy setting must not quietly turn durability off.
INBOX_DEDUP_MS=300000             # a repeat of the same delivery inside this
                                  # window is a retry, not a second event
INBOX_MAX_AGE_MS=86400000         # a delivery still unfinished after this is
                                  # dropped rather than run

# ─── Secret store ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Master key for credentials kept in the database instead of here, so adding
# or rotating one is `bun run secret -- set KEY` rather than a redeploy.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32   (32 bytes, base64 or hex)
#
# Unset, the store is inert and every credential comes from this file, exactly
# as it did before the store existed. A stored value WINS over the same name
# set here — that is what makes rotating one not need a deploy.
SECRETS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# How often a running server notices a `bun run secret` write made by another
# process. An API write is applied immediately and doesn't wait for this.
SECRET_REFRESH_MS=10000           # 0 disables polling entirely

# ─── OAuth2 credentials (defineOAuth) ───────────────────────────────────
# Only needed if a workflow declares one. Master key for the refresh tokens
# kept in state — encrypted alongside the secret store above. Falls back to
# SECRETS_ENCRYPTION_KEY's counterpart: set either, or both to separate them.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32   (32 bytes, base64 or hex)
OAUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Per credential, named after it: defineOAuth("notion", …) reads OAUTH_NOTION_*.
# The refresh token is obtained once, out of band — there is no consent flow
# here — and is replaced by the rotated one from then on.
# OAUTH_NOTION_CLIENT_ID=
# OAUTH_NOTION_CLIENT_SECRET=
# OAUTH_NOTION_REFRESH_TOKEN=

# ─── Durable state (ctx.state) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Key/value store that outlives runs. Never rendered on the dashboard or API,
# and — unlike everything else on disk — stored as given, not redacted.
STATE_MAX_BYTES=262144            # per state value

# ─── Access ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Without these the dashboard is public. Set them before exposing the port.
DASHBOARD_USER=admin
DASHBOARD_PASS=change-me

# Lets the Credentials tab add, edit, test and delete credentials from the
# browser. Off, that tab is a read-only view and every write route answers 403,
# which is how the whole dashboard behaved before the tab existed.
#
# Worth turning on when you write workflows with an AI coding agent: the agent
# writes the defineCredential() line, you paste the value into the form, and the
# credential never passes through a terminal command or a chat transcript. That
# is hygiene, not a boundary — anything that can read this file can decrypt the
# store regardless.
#
# The CLI and the API write whether this is set or not; it only governs the
# browser.
DASHBOARD_WRITE=0

# Required on every /hooks/* call as X-Automator-Secret, Bearer token, or ?secret=
WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# The canonical external URL of this runner. Used to link back to a run from a
# failure alert, and as the base for webhooks that register themselves with
# their provider — without it, no subscription is created or changed.
# e.g. https://automator.example.com
PUBLIC_URL=

# Where the runner tells you it has a problem — a run that used up its retries,
# a run refused because a credential is not connected, a workflow that would not
# load, a webhook delivery turned away at the door.
#
# <platform> or <platform>:<credential>[/<where>], where <credential> is one you
# connected on the Credentials tab, so the token stays in the encrypted store
# rather than in this file:
#
#   telegram                  whichever Telegram credential is primary
#   telegram:the-mantra       that bot, the chat id that credential carries
#   telegram:the-mantra/-100… that bot, a specific chat
#   slack:ops/#alerts         that bot token, that channel
#   discord:ops               that credential's incoming webhook
#   webhook:https://…         any Slack- or Discord-shaped incoming webhook
#
# Every workflow is connected unless its file says `alerts: false`.
ALERT_CHANNEL=

# How long the same problem stays quiet after being reported once. The next
# alert that gets through carries the count of what it stands for. 0 sends
# everything. Default 1800000 (30 minutes).
ALERT_COOLDOWN_MS=

# The knob ALERT_CHANNEL replaced. Still honoured, and means
# ALERT_CHANNEL=webhook:<this url>.
ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=

# ─── Integrations (only set what your workflows actually declare) ───────
# Everything below can instead be a credential created on the Credentials tab.
# A credential marked "use this for the built-in client" fills these same names
# at boot, and wins over whatever is set here — which is what lets you rotate an
# SMTP password without touching this file.
# Several accounts for one service: defineSecretGroup("GITHUB_TOKEN", …) picks
# up every GITHUB_TOKEN_* below as { personal, work, client_acme }.
# GITHUB_TOKEN_PERSONAL=
# GITHUB_TOKEN_WORK=

SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=
# Huzaifah's own chat id — where both the pblsh and The Mantra alerts land.
# Configuration rather than a secret, so it is read straight from the
# environment and stays readable on the run page. Each of those workflows sends
# from its own brand's bot but to this one destination; unset, each falls back
# to the chat id stored on its own Telegram credential.
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID_HUZAIFAH=
# Jenny's chat id, for the Contents notifier. Same reasoning as above, with one
# difference: this one has no fallback to the bot's own default chat, because
# "send Jenny's queue to whoever the default is" is worse than not sending it.
# The workflow ships with her id as the literal default, so leaving this unset
# works — set it only when the person changes.
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID_JENNY=
DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL=

ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=

SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=
SMTP_FROM=

POSTGRES_URL=

# The full service-account JSON on one line. Share the sheet — and any Drive
# file ctx.drive must read — with its client_email. The service account is its
# own identity, not the person whose Drive the folder lives in, so a file nobody
# shared with it comes back as a 404 rather than as a permission error.
GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON=

# ctx.s3 — S3-compatible object storage, written for Cloudflare R2. Used to give
# a public URL to media that another service has to fetch for itself.
# Everything here can instead be the "S3 / Cloudflare R2" credential on the
# Credentials tab, which is the better home: only two of the six are secrets and
# the tab keeps those in the encrypted store.
#
# THE BUCKET MUST BE PUBLICLY READABLE. That is the whole point — the URL is
# handed to a third party with no credentials — and it is why anything uploaded
# is expected to be deleted again as soon as the fetch is done.
S3_ENDPOINT=                      # https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
S3_BUCKET=
S3_PUBLIC_URL=                    # https://pub-<hash>.r2.dev
S3_REGION=                        # optional; R2 wants the literal "auto"

# ─── PBLSH ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# workflows/pblsh/send-signed-agreement.ts. Brevo's transactional API key
# (xkeysib-…), declared with defineSecrets — unset, the boot stops here.
BREVO_API_KEY=
# Tally's signing secret for the form. Tally HMACs the body with this and
# sends only the digest, so WEBHOOK_SECRET cannot authenticate it — the
# trigger uses tallySignature() instead. Unset, the boot stops here.
TALLY_SIGNING_SECRET=
# The bot that sends the "agreement signed" ping is a *credential*, not a
# variable here. Connect it on the Credentials tab as Telegram / "pblsh".
# Unset, the whole workflow is blocked — a Tally submission is refused at the
# door and the creator never gets their PDF, so connect it before deploying.
# It goes to TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID_HUZAIFAH, above.
#
# PBLSH_TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID was documented here and read by nothing. Delete it
# from your .env.

# ─── The Mantra ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# workflows/the-mantra/threads-content-runway-alert.ts reads both of its
# platforms through *credentials*, not variables here. Connect them on the
# Credentials tab:
#
#   Notion   / "the-mantra-contents"  an internal integration token (ntn_…)
#                                     with read access to the Threads database
#   Telegram / "the-mantra"           the bot the runway nudge is sent from
#
# Unset, the alert is marked blocked and refuses to run; it does not stop the
# boot, which is what lets you connect it from the page. The nudge goes to
# TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID_HUZAIFAH, above.
#
# NOTION_API_KEY and MANTRA_TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID used to live here and are read by
# nothing now. Delete them from your .env once the credentials are connected.
#
# workflows/the-mantra/threads-token-auto-refresh.ts keeps the long-lived
# Threads token alive. Unlike the credentials above, this one is a *secret* and
# a missing one stops the boot — set it before you deploy the file, which the
# store lets you do ahead of time:
#
#   bun run secret -- set OAUTH_THREADS_HUZAIFAH_REFRESH_TOKEN
#
# It also needs OAUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEY, above, and the Telegram / "the-mantra"
# credential, shared with the runway alert.
#
# Paste the long-lived token you got from the Threads auth flow. From then on
# the workflow replaces it weekly and this variable is only the seed — pasting
# a new one abandons the stored chain, which is exactly how you recover after a
# token dies. It must have been issued with the threads_delete scope.
# OAUTH_THREADS_HUZAIFAH_REFRESH_TOKEN=

# workflows/the-mantra/notion-contents-update-notification.ts receives Notion's
# webhook at POST /hooks/the-mantra/notion-contents. It reuses the Notion /
# "the-mantra-contents" and Telegram / "the-mantra" credentials above and adds
# one secret, which is *optional* and cannot be set in advance:
#
#   NOTION_WEBHOOK_TOKEN
#
# Notion mints it, POSTs it once to the endpoint you are subscribing, and never
# shows it again. So the route has to exist before the token can, and a required
# secret here would mean the deploy that creates the endpoint is the deploy that
# cannot boot. Unset, every real event is rejected and only the handshake gets
# through.
#
# The one-time setup, after deploying. The order matters — see step 0:
#
#   0. Connect Notion / "the-mantra-contents" and Telegram / "the-mantra" on the
#      Credentials tab FIRST. An unconnected credential blocks the whole
#      workflow, so a handshake that arrives before them is refused and the
#      token is dropped — Notion still gets its 202 and shows the delivery as
#      made, and all you get is a red run saying "Not connected".
#   1. Add the subscription in Notion (Integrations → your integration →
#      Webhooks) pointing at https://<PUBLIC_URL>/hooks/the-mantra/notion-contents
#   2. The token arrives on Telegram, in the message. Paste it into Notion's
#      verification modal — the subscription goes Active.
#   3. Paste the same value on the dashboard under Secrets → New, named
#      NOTION_WEBHOOK_TOKEN. It takes effect on the next delivery, no restart.
#
# No shell, no container, no database — the whole thing is a Telegram message,
# Notion's modal and one dashboard form, so it is not a job that has to come
# back to whoever has SSH.
#
# Missed the message, or need to start over? "Resend token" in Notion's
# verification modal sends the whole thing again — but wait out INBOX_DEDUP_MS
# (default 5 minutes) first, because Notion resends a byte-identical body and
# the inbox treats that as a retry and drops it.
#
# The token is also kept in ctx.state under
# "notion:the-mantra-contents:verification-token", as the copy of last resort.
# Nothing renders state, so reading it needs the server:
#
#   docker compose exec -T automator bun -e 'import{Database}from"bun:sqlite"; \
#     const r=new Database(process.env.DATABASE_PATH??"/data/automator.db", \
#     {readonly:true}).query("select value from state where namespace=? and \
#     key=?").get("@shared","notion:the-mantra-contents:verification-token"); \
#     console.log(r?JSON.parse(r.value):"(not found)")'
#
# Not sqlite3: the runtime image is bun:1-alpine plus tini and has no CLI.
# NOTION_WEBHOOK_TOKEN=

# workflows/the-mantra/notion-contents-telegram-commands.ts answers Telegram
# commands at POST /hooks/the-mantra/telegram-commands. It reuses the Notion /
# "the-mantra-contents" and Telegram / "the-mantra" credentials above and adds
# one secret, which — unlike NOTION_WEBHOOK_TOKEN — is *required* and *yours*:
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#   bun run secret -- set TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET
#
# You pick the value, it is handed to Telegram's setWebhook as `secret_token`,
# and Telegram sends it back on every delivery. So it can exist before the route
# does, and a missing one stops the boot rather than leaving a bot that
# registers a webhook and then rejects everything it receives.
TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Who may command the bot — n8n's chat allowlist. Comma-separated Telegram chat
# ids; anything from elsewhere is dropped without a reply. Configuration, not a
# secret, so it stays readable on the run page. The workflow ships with
# Huzaifah's and Jenny's ids as the default, so leaving this unset works.
TELEGRAM_COMMAND_CHAT_IDS=
#
# PUBLIC_URL must be set, above: the workflow registers its own webhook, and
# without it nothing is registered and the bot never hears anything.
#
# ONE BOT, ONE WEBHOOK. Telegram allows a single webhook per bot, and booting
# this workflow calls setWebhook. The Mantra bot stops delivering to n8n at that
# moment — there is no way to run both, and nothing warns you. Delete the n8n
# workflow once this one answers.

# workflows/the-mantra/threads-poster.ts posts due Approved threads. It adds no
# variables at all — it reuses the Notion / "the-mantra-contents" and Telegram /
# "the-mantra" credentials and the OAUTH_THREADS_HUZAIFAH_REFRESH_TOKEN seed
# above — but three things about it are worth knowing before you deploy:
#
#   1. The Notion integration now needs WRITE access to the Threads database,
#      not just read. It sets Status, Log and Thread URL. A read-only share
#      fails on the update, after the thread is already live.
#   2. The Threads token must have been issued to the account in
#      THREADS_USER_ID in the file. A token for a different account posts to
#      that account and nothing about the error will say so.
#   3. NOTHING STOPS BOTH THIS AND n8n POSTING. Unlike the Telegram bot, which
#      physically cannot deliver to two places, this reads Notion on a timer —
#      so if the n8n "The Mantra - Threads - Checker" is still active when this
#      deploys, every due row goes out twice. Deactivate the n8n workflow
#      first, and check the Threads account after the first run.

# workflows/the-mantra/cross-poster.ts cross-posts a Contents row that already
# went out on TikTok to Instagram, Facebook and Threads. It replaces three n8n
# graphs: "Cross-post Checker", "Cross-post (IG, FB & Threads)" and its error
# handler.
#
# It adds NO variables of its own. Everything it needs is a credential on the
# Credentials tab plus one OAuth seed:
#
#   Notion   / "the-mantra-contents"  shared with the runway alert and the
#                                     Threads poster. Needs WRITE access — it
#                                     sets Status, ticks the Postings to-dos,
#                                     and appends the checklist to old pages.
#   Telegram / "the-mantra"           the bot the failure alert is sent from
#   Meta     / "the-mantra"           the Page access token, Page ID and
#                                     Instagram user ID (see below)
#   S3       / "the-mantra"           the R2 bucket media is staged in
#
#   bun run secret -- set OAUTH_THREADS_THE_MANTRA_REFRESH_TOKEN
#
# Six things to know before deploying it:
#
#   1. ROTATE THE FACEBOOK PAGE TOKEN FIRST. The n8n version had it pasted in
#      plain text into two HTTP nodes, so it is in n8n's database, in every
#      execution record it ever made, and in the exported JSON. Treat it as
#      compromised: issue a new one and put that on the Credentials tab.
#   2. The Meta token must be a LONG-LIVED PAGE token derived from a long-lived
#      user token — those do not expire. A short-lived one dies in an hour and
#      nothing here refreshes it. The credential's Test button reports the
#      expiry it sees, and "token does not expire" is what you want.
#   3. The Instagram account must be a BUSINESS account linked to that Page, or
#      publishing answers 400 with nothing that says why.
#   4. Share the Google Drive folder the File(s) links point at with the
#      service account's client_email, or every row fails on download.
#   5. NOTHING STOPS BOTH THIS AND n8n POSTING. Like the Threads poster, this
#      reads Notion on a timer — so if the n8n "Cross-post Checker" is still
#      active when this deploys, every due row goes out twice, to three
#      platforms. Deactivate the n8n workflows first.
#   6. A row is only picked up between 3 and 10 days after its TikTok post
#      date. Rows that age past 10 days are never cross-posted — inherited from
#      n8n on purpose, because widening the window would back-post a pile of old
#      content on the first deploy. They are no longer silent: see the stale
#      alert below.
#
# The brand Threads token is kept alive by
# workflows/the-mantra/threads-token-auto-refresh.ts, which now refreshes both
# accounts — the founder's and this one — in one weekly run. The n8n cross-post
# graph had no refresher at all and said so in a sticky note; that was a 60-day
# fuse. Both seeds must be set or the boot stops.
# OAUTH_THREADS_THE_MANTRA_REFRESH_TOKEN=

# workflows/the-mantra/cross-post-stale-alert.ts reports content that will never
# cross-post — rows that aged past the cross-poster's 10-day ceiling, and rows
# left stuck on Status = "Posting" after a run died holding the lock. Both are
# invisible otherwise: nothing retries them and nothing complains.
#
# It adds NO variables. It reuses the Notion / "the-mantra-contents" and
# Telegram / "the-mantra" credentials and sends to TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID_HUZAIFAH.
# Read-only against Notion — it reports, it never fixes.
#
# Runs 09:00 KL daily, and it does not nag: a message when something new turns
# up, then quiet, then one reminder a week while anything is still outstanding.
# Trigger it by hand to see the current state whatever it has said before:
#
#   bun run trigger -- the-mantra-cross-post-stale-alert
#
# MAX_AGE_DAYS is duplicated in that file from cross-poster.ts, because a
# workflow cannot import another workflow. Change the window in one and change
# it in the other — a mismatch shows up as the alert going quiet, which is
# exactly what it will not announce.
